---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import BlogPostHeader from '../../components/BlogPostHeader.astro';
import { currentMonthYear } from '../../utils/site-date';

const title = "What is POPLA and How Do I Use It? | AppealAirportPCN";
const description = "A plain-English guide to POPLA, who can use it, how to submit a second-stage appeal, and what to check if an operator rejects your airport PCN appeal.";
const canonical = "https://appealairportpcn.co.uk/blog/what-is-popla/";

const schema = {
  "@context": "https://schema.org",
  "@type": "Article",
  "headline": "What is POPLA and how do I use it?",
  "description": description,
  "author": { "@type": "Organization", "name": "AppealAirportPCN" },
  "publisher": { "@type": "Organization", "name": "AppealAirportPCN.co.uk" },
  "datePublished": "2026-04-15",
  "dateModified": "2026-10-05"
};
---

<BaseLayout title={title} description={description} canonical={canonical} schema={schema}>
  <BlogPostHeader title="What is POPLA and how do I use it?" breadcrumbLabel="What is POPLA?" breadcrumbHref="/blog/what-is-popla/" updatedDate={currentMonthYear.toUpperCase()} />
  <main>
    <div class="wrap">
      <article class="blog-post">
        <p>If a parking company rejects your first appeal, check the rejection letter before paying. You may be able to make a second appeal through <strong>POPLA</strong>, an independent appeals service. It is free to use.</p>

        <h2>What POPLA is</h2>
        <p>POPLA stands for <strong>Parking on Private Land Appeals</strong>. An assessor reviews information from you and the parking company and makes a decision on the appeal.</p>
        <p>When POPLA allows an appeal, the parking company must follow that decision and cancel the charge. Check the current <a href="https://www.popla.co.uk/appeal-guide" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">POPLA appeal guide</a> for details about the process.</p>

        <h2>Who can use POPLA?</h2>
        <p>POPLA is generally available when the operator is a member of the <strong>British Parking Association (BPA)</strong>. Check your PCN and rejection letter to see which trade body and appeal service apply.</p>
        <p>If the operator belongs to the <strong>International Parking Community (IPC)</strong>, its second-stage service is the <strong>Independent Appeals Service (IAS)</strong>. The service and rules differ, so check the information on your notice.</p>

        <h2>Get your POPLA code</h2>
        <p>If a BPA-member operator rejects your appeal, its rejection should include a POPLA verification code. If you cannot find one, contact the operator and ask how to make your independent appeal.</p>

        <h2>How to submit</h2>
        <ol>
          <li>Go to <a href="https://www.popla.co.uk/" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">popla.co.uk</a>.</li>
          <li>Enter the verification code from the rejection letter.</li>
          <li>Explain your grounds and upload any supporting evidence.</li>
          <li>Submit your appeal before the deadline shown in the rejection letter.</li>
        </ol>
        <p>Check the deadline on your letter and the current POPLA instructions. Do not assume the same time limit applies to every appeal route.</p>

        <h2>What to include</h2>
        <p>Use the points that apply to your case. For example:</p>
        <ul>
          <li>The land may not be "relevant land" under <a href="/blog/what-is-pofa-and-why-it-matters/">POFA Schedule 4</a>.</li>
          <li>The signs may not meet the <a href="/blog/bpa-code-of-practice-signage/">BPA Code of Practice</a>.</li>
          <li>The vehicle may have left within the <a href="/blog/five-minute-consideration-period/">five-minute consideration period</a>.</li>
          <li>The charge may not have been shown before the contract was formed. Read about <a href="/blog/thornton-v-shoe-lane-parking/">Thornton v Shoe Lane Parking</a>.</li>
        </ul>
        <p>Include relevant evidence, such as photos of signs, ANPR images, or a map of the approach road. Keep copies of everything you send.</p>

        <h2>How long it takes</h2>
        <p>POPLA will provide information about the expected timescale and send its decision when the assessment is complete. Check your email and the POPLA portal for updates.</p>

        <h2>If your appeal is not allowed</h2>
        <p>Read the decision and consider your options. POPLA is an alternative dispute process, not a court. If you are unsure what to do next, seek advice from a solicitor or Citizens Advice. Our guide explains <a href="/blog/what-responses-to-expect/">what to expect after an appeal</a>.</p>

        <div class="disclaimer">
          <strong>Disclaimer:</strong> This article is for information only and does not constitute legal advice. For complex cases, consult a solicitor or Citizens Advice.
        </div>
      </article>
    </div>
  </main>
</BaseLayout>
